FG-054: EOT decoding is on only when the HOST asks, never because of the document.

libeot is built into the presentations module (--enable-eot). This makes embeddedfontshelper decode an
embedded EOT font only when the engine's environment has LO_WASM_ALLOW_EOT=1; otherwise the font is
treated exactly as in a build without EOT (not decoded, not used, its temporary file removed).

The converter's worker sets the variable in preRun when INIT carries allowEmbeddedEot === true
(web/js/conversion_worker.js). The website's driver sends it; the MCP builds INIT itself from a fixed
field list and sends nothing, so it keeps an engine that refuses EOT (the WAF's condition, 2026-10-24,
docs/converter-contract.md F-CC-006 in the MCP repo). Anything but exactly "1" refuses.

Module-scoped (-presentations): ENABLE_EOT is off in every other module, so this code is not compiled
there.

Rights (owner decision 2026-10-29): an EOT font follows the same rights rule as every other embedded font, not
libeot's EOTcanLegallyEdit (installable or editable only), which stays in use outside WASM. Preview & Print is
accepted for every output, the least restrictive usage bit wins, Restricted alone and bitmap-only are refused, and
no-subsetting refuses PDF output only. The rule is applied to the fsType in the EOT header (lcl_eotHeaderRights,
the same decision order as lcl_sufficientRights) AND to the decoded font's own OS/2 fsType
(lcl_sufficientRights); both must pass. The converter's font extractor sees only the header, so requiring both
means it can never skip a substitute download for a font the engine refuses; a header that allows what the font
forbids costs one extra conversion (the driver's verify-and-retry). The font file that is written is the decoded
font itself, so a re-embedded font keeps its real fsType.

diff --git a/vcl/source/gdi/embeddedfontshelper.cxx b/vcl/source/gdi/embeddedfontshelper.cxx
--- a/vcl/source/gdi/embeddedfontshelper.cxx
+++ b/vcl/source/gdi/embeddedfontshelper.cxx
@@ -31,6 +31,7 @@
 #include <sft.hxx>
 
 #if ENABLE_EOT
+#include <cstdlib>
 extern "C"
 {
 namespace libeot
@@ -38,6 +39,43 @@
 #include <libeot/libeot.h>
 } // namespace libeot
 } // extern "C"
+
+// FG-054: whether EOT is decoded is decided by the EMBEDDER, never by the document. The converter's
+// browser driver asks for it (INIT.allowEmbeddedEot); its worker then sets LO_WASM_ALLOW_EOT=1 in the
+// engine's environment before the engine starts. A host that does not (the MCP) gets the engine as it
+// is without EOT: the font is not decoded and not used. Anything but exactly "1" refuses.
+static bool lcl_eotAllowed()
+{
+#ifdef __EMSCRIPTEN__
+    const char* p = getenv( "LO_WASM_ALLOW_EOT" );
+    return p && p[0] == '1' && p[1] == '\0';
+#else
+    return true;
+#endif
+}
+
+#ifdef __EMSCRIPTEN__
+// Owner decision 2026-10-29: an EOT font follows the same rights rule as every other embedded font. This is
+// that rule (lcl_sufficientRights, below, is the same decision order) for the fsType in the EOT header, which
+// the converter's font extractor also reads. The engine requires the header AND the decoded font's own OS/2
+// fsType to pass, so the extractor, which sees only the header, can never skip a substitute download for a
+// font the engine then refuses unseen: the only disagreement left is a header that allows what the font
+// forbids, which costs one extra conversion (the driver's verify-and-retry), never a missing face.
+static bool lcl_eotHeaderRights( sal_uInt32 nFsType, bool bPdfOnly )
+{
+    if( nFsType & 0x0200 )
+        return false; // bitmap embedding only
+    if( bPdfOnly && ( nFsType & 0x0100 ) )
+        return false; // no subsetting
+    if( nFsType & 0x0008 )
+        return true; // Editable (wins over 0x0002 and 0x0004)
+    if( nFsType & 0x0004 )
+        return true; // Preview & Print: every output
+    if( nFsType & 0x0002 )
+        return false; // Restricted alone
+    return true; // Installable
+}
+#endif
 #endif
 
 using namespace com::sun::star;
@@ -165,7 +203,7 @@
     }
     bool sufficientFontRights(false);
 #if ENABLE_EOT
-    if( eot )
+    if( eot && lcl_eotAllowed() )
     {
         unsigned uncompressedFontSize = 0;
         unsigned char *nakedPointerToUncompressedFont = nullptr;
@@ -191,7 +229,17 @@
             }
             writtenTotal += written;
         }
+#ifdef __EMSCRIPTEN__
+        // Owner decision 2026-10-29: the same rights rule as every other embedded font (the 2026-10-22 rule:
+        // Preview & Print counts for every output, the least restrictive usage bit wins, Restricted alone and
+        // bitmap-only are refused, no-subsetting refuses PDF only), applied to the EOT header's fsType and to
+        // the decoded font's own OS/2 fsType; both must pass. libeot's EOTcanLegallyEdit (installable or
+        // editable only) is not used here.
+        sufficientFontRights = lcl_eotHeaderRights( eotMetadata.permissions, bPdfOnly )
+            && lcl_sufficientRights( uncompressedFont.get(), uncompressedFontSize, bPdfOnly );
+#else
         sufficientFontRights = libeot::EOTcanLegallyEdit( &eotMetadata );
+#endif
         libeot::EOTfreeMetadata( &eotMetadata );
     }
 #endif
